What:		/sys/fs/nfs/net/nfs_client/shutdown
Date:		September 2026
Contact:	vpsAdminOS developers <jakub.skokan@vpsfree.cz>
Description:
		Host-controlled, terminal cancellation of NFS client activity in
		the network namespace selected by the sysfs mount. Mode is 0600.
		Reading returns 0 before cancellation and 1 after cancellation.
		Writing the integer 1 marks NFS clients and volumes shut down,
		wakes initialization/recovery waiters and cancels client SUNRPC
		tasks with EIO. Subsequent client RPC admission is terminally
		cancelled, including clients created by a concurrent mount.

		Writes require CAP_SYS_ADMIN in the initial user namespace.
		Network namespaces owned by the initial user namespace are
		rejected with EPERM, including non-initial network namespaces.
		Values other than 1 are rejected with EINVAL. Repeated writes
		of 1 are permitted. There is no operation to undo cancellation.

		This is a teardown operation, not a retry policy or a substitute
		for sync/unmount. It can discard pending writes and does not
		wait for all tasks, mounts or namespace references to drain.
		It cancels all client SUNRPC activity in the namespace, not
		only RPC belonging to one NFS mount (for example, NLM and
		rpcbind client calls are also affected). NFS server activity is
		not itself shut down. Other network namespaces are not selected.
		Create fresh namespaces before restarting the workload.

What:		/sys/fs/nfs/net/nfs_client/shutdown_tree
Date:		September 2026
Contact:	vpsAdminOS developers <jakub.skokan@vpsfree.cz>
Description:
		Host-controlled, terminal cancellation of client SUNRPC and NFS
		for the user namespace owning the network namespace selected by
		the sysfs mount, and all descendants of that user namespace.
		The permissions, accepted write value, error handling, data-loss
		warning and non-waiting semantics are as for shutdown above.

		Writing 1 installs a permanent user-namespace admission barrier
		before cancelling existing clients and marking NFS volumes shut
		down. The scope includes every network namespace owned by that
		user namespace or a descendant, even if no process occupies it
		or its creation is still in progress. Future network namespaces
		and future descendant user namespaces inherit the barrier.
		Sibling or ancestor user namespaces are not selected.

		Reading returns 1 when the owner or any ancestor carries this
		barrier, otherwise 0. Cancelling only one network namespace via
		shutdown does not set the tree barrier. There is no reset: a
		restarted workload needs a fresh user namespace outside the
		cancelled subtree, as well as fresh network namespaces.

		The caller must authenticate namespace ownership and select
		the intended teardown root. Two workloads sharing the same
		owner user namespace cannot be isolated by this operation.
